#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180553} :activity/id #uuid "c9655728-f410-56c7-8943-6d053246e392" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049035 :topic/id #uuid "d53c1458-9af5-589b-8d7b-d3dbe4e7a946" :topic/math-academy-id 3706} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180754 :tutorial/content "To find the image of a point under an affine transformation, all we need to do is substitute the position vector of the point into the formula for the affine transformation.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that the point $P({\\color{black}-1},{\\color{black}1})$ with position vector $\\mathbf p$ lies in the $uv$-plane, as shown below. \n\n![](images/06/06c9283bde54e329d31d5892702e4a75c0ca78bd21ee3bb23f9a2b748239bf14.png)\n\nWe can find the image of $P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by substituting $\\mathbf p$ into the affine transformation formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(P) & =Ap+b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $P$ is the point $P'({\\color{black}-1},{\\color{black}3})$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe can visualize $P$ and its image under $\\mathbf T$ using a diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/89/893933990225c54e0a3327de70f29b3d73af2b829cf53053d155bb52d5202e63.png)\n\nWe can use a similar method to map multiple points from the $uv$-plane to the $xy$-plane using just one calculation. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "b71d5e38-7267-5428-89f5-941492316127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7694 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7e42cdff-0162-5f00-9062-0bbf4e7ef4c8" :step/math-academy-id 44648 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179428 :question/id #uuid "102d79a9-f19c-5735-a3f2-347938820b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-12425"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ba09dd1-d219-5df2-adfa-d88d7b9fc481" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417836}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Point or Segment"} :step/id #uuid "130575e3-c9fd-5688-a557-9be7454f3b61" :step/math-academy-id 44649 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180755 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle $\\Delta$ with sides $S_1, S_2,$ and $S_3,$  shown below. Let's imagine that we traverse the boundary once in the counterclockwise direction starting from the origin.\n\n![](images/23/231dd5f96cd36d21940fcee894382edc9eabc91b0bf72bb4a8724c8444f76a8c.png)\n\nLet the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle, presented in the order shown in the diagram:\n![](images/34/34be6d7cd24938fb72976910cf3ad6a790eb93574915829f621da99f4aec3838.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/be/bed30fcc6a1003bee7f2f7888af9c301314b2aba351497db35cf2ee37710c0a1.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertices of the image are given by the columns of the above matrix. Drawing these vertices in the order shown, i.e.\n\n$$\n(1,-1) \\to (1,0) \\to (2,-1),\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture:\n\n![](images/d5/d514834e6e9f108f478002416e863cf9c98d00add230c2e9321664ea14e5db0e.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that the polygon $D$ is traversed in the *clockwise* direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "c717442d-1c85-597b-bfc9-3bacde35a1f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7695 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "804e3ae7-0f27-5ff7-9101-bf7c7b3dfa01" :step/math-academy-id 44652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180756 :tutorial/content "An invertible affine transformation is **orientation-preserving** if it preserves the orientation of any polygon in the plane.\n\nTo understand this, let's go back to our triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)\n\nThis transformation does *not* preserve orientation because when we traverse $\\Delta$ *counterclockwise*, its image $D$ is traversed *clockwise.*\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation is orientation-preserving or not by examining the matrix $A$ of the associated linear map. We have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is defined as* \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Then, $\\mathbf{T}$ preserves orientation if*\n\n$$\n\\det(A) > 0,\n$$\n\n*and does not preserve orientation if*\n\n$$\n\\det(A) < 0.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to cases where $\\det(A) < 0$ as **orientation-reversing.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "0444feba-dbdb-5165-942a-79da29a810e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7696 :tutorial/title "Orientation-Preserving Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "3f2a959e-2593-532e-916d-db411d030416" :step/math-academy-id 44653 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179429 :question/id #uuid "290ea83a-1a47-5260-82b2-346d38e9adcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12426"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387526}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "bfcdd1b6-82a4-5529-8d93-b859895ed88c" :step/math-academy-id 44654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179430 :question/id #uuid "0f180b16-c807-555d-b92c-826a216a31e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12427"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371700}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"} :step/id #uuid "db19ad89-86be-5bd4-8aba-82b5b0c23c42" :step/math-academy-id 44657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179434 :question/id #uuid "e6f17219-642b-51d3-bebe-61519ee6651f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12436"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049339}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417837}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6e8df5a8-690a-5bd7-acaa-c40815a5fca6" :step/math-academy-id 44685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180760 :tutorial/content "Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$  is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nSimilar to the case of linear transformations, the ** area scale factor** of $\\mathbf T$ is given by the determinant of $A.$\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation increases or decreases the area of a polygon $\\mathcal P$ by examining the area scale factor:\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|>1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *larger* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|=1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have the *same* area as $\\mathcal P.$\n\n- If $0 < |\\det(A)| < 1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *smaller* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\nNote that if $\\det(A) = 0,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ is not a polygon. We discuss this case in more detail in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2,$ from the previous discussion, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/7c/7c637211f31b2e8e49fb56f6e60573c47bada4fa36b6c6e0fa7474c3e4d106fc.svg)\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |-1| = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the area of triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ under $\\mathbf T$ in the $xy$-plane have the same area. \n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e306ae2c-f307-52ed-a9fc-9376bdfeaa2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7705 :tutorial/title "The Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "0c8f36a3-dfdb-59b6-82cb-0413df0361cd" :step/math-academy-id 44703 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180564}}] :activity/title "The Image of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179859}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408041} :activity/id #uuid "eb78a48d-c31f-5955-bc9e-cdc4e0762d1c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049035 :topic/id #uuid "d53c1458-9af5-589b-8d7b-d3dbe4e7a946" :topic/math-academy-id 3706} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbf8e90-0fcd-5e9d-ad8e-5d029388c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257325} {:answer/id #uuid "43d247fa-abed-5958-b466-10fc02c6fd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257379} {:answer/id #uuid "4681c1f9-2a9f-5136-9cf7-ed7506a00643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0eefab-460e-5b30-b83c-d3d6a7dee230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257593} {:answer/id #uuid "7277e3e9-81db-502b-97dc-3210914ad7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257325} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced03044-c848-5e8d-a236-e8367ae6dd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6b75fa0-1178-5417-bc31-861bbdef5229" :question/math-academy-id "q-124321" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v-u+2,\\,y=2u+v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-5$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v-u+2 \\\\ 2u+v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2=-5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0a78ca60-8278-5f69-a0c5-4853a7109868" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccab3c0-0254-5090-9c86-829a851b15b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256907} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4c2cb2-f173-5288-817b-371883d27529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257179} {:answer/id #uuid "60740839-3d41-5df6-8849-4084e44e6ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257630} {:answer/id #uuid "b74a87c3-7a0f-5446-af5b-58a1007116ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258382} {:answer/id #uuid "d661a75a-735f-5ece-a15b-6a8a834246d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258382} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b4cd81-c3a3-52e0-a0d4-355e8ea3aed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0a1ba57-9eac-5d49-9cdd-f7f25fec8c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-124192" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v+u+1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-3$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v+u+1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "88a789c1-e3c2-5586-9aab-92005c1e4bcd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75895875-a73e-5cbb-bf3d-c5aa2a0d474a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d1/d1e93d9ce18d7c75ab6432d53ae9ff72fd3fe2c89125a12dcd2d41a13100465d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257813} {:answer/id #uuid "7e96b286-9f53-5a16-a78e-11d14b7729bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/8254c51a8f1c9f0ca48f169fb48a938b07e5812b5be866d9ab25e5fbdffed368.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257908} {:answer/id #uuid "8db86e0e-3c7f-5fc7-8dd6-cb016f66e98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6b/6be30b91e0526e28530b4c5cfff6c0c5e7be53a77e587c5eb37a845b6cc0a189.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258028} {:answer/id #uuid "aac5b30f-88de-5a6d-a659-c1f1d57058a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a7/a7b171394aeccd9b807fc29ecf7c33f745c58bce3ed01bcf879a79d2edf97319.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258271} {:answer/id #uuid "c68d0b11-70cb-55fa-8c98-86952dd0839e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/27/27dbdbec3125bdef5109bf6aa549c9bcfe83175b42e660cc44977540de0ad112.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258517}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257813} :answer-field/id #uuid "047ad574-9503-5413-9fac-632c989f1e97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fdc3f93-8486-56f6-8744-50fa49ca621b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124561" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09bcf02998adf6d7bf19e7d59cabdc563ff26266966440bbb49be53029ac3.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{u+1}{2}\\le v\\le u+1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/53/539c74bf4d397916fd7ac11089fa9500bd71cdf2278891136785d6e792431b05.png)\n\n             \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,-1),(1,-1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/ad/adfe476bd2ca719996d612855ca5368bdea09370b7befcffb08a062db8587cbb.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving."} :step/id #uuid "de3c23cf-80a3-5af4-81aa-d8176ded4ab6"}] :activity/title "The Image of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408039}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435513} :activity/id #uuid "6576ae72-76b4-5f19-bc0f-f5d6a65eb6eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049035 :topic/id #uuid "d53c1458-9af5-589b-8d7b-d3dbe4e7a946" :topic/math-academy-id 3706} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124ccea5-c558-54e8-931d-ac8f24d5e647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256949} {:answer/id #uuid "91981231-c419-532a-b1a0-f3d52aacc432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(13,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258068} {:answer/id #uuid "9389800b-ab1e-59a9-990e-685c251fb1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(11,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258082} {:answer/id #uuid "c439a84e-99e8-5cc9-a96d-e5fe22288db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(11,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258499} {:answer/id #uuid "f08db5c5-f719-59c7-88c9-4c17884b91d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256949} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cac29fa-2311-5bc0-9f73-f59e7d67297f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fea0aad7-f097-5052-bac7-eb29df7cbe2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124101" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u+v-2,\\,y=u-2v+1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(3,2)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v-2 \\\\ u-2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(9,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "7a0b96ab-0e8f-5f84-b9de-02610ea22220" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1283e1d9-5638-5cf5-8afe-fccb48c4513e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278761} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e99c71-ce7b-5a57-8b35-eff03f28886a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280088} {:answer/id #uuid "bee5e71a-fc3b-5642-9353-bcf840af6970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280271} {:answer/id #uuid "d650e02b-bf8f-5b88-a546-cd6aa55f62f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280483} {:answer/id #uuid "e733f81f-7e5b-56da-a2cd-22f5cd7afff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280483} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5d13f4-2cb9-5ffa-b608-20e5bb1d0372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79b6b1e2-201b-5b88-aae8-abebb7edf5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162195" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-2v-3,\\,y=u-v+2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-2v-3 \\\\ u-v+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|0|=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e6a316c-c2fc-577f-ab80-35087f6f4a4b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e0716d6-0de1-55dd-aa8f-f9f8c7dfcca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e1/e181f53d2f99a2374739769ea5e5837de0acf8526717f0607e0e81dba4da0c1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387521} {:answer/id #uuid "977680f2-b11c-5c52-b5b6-570b836e607f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a1/a1c56986c6de278a24b0d83d69b26822b4330d815b8f501db7da8f3d10e625da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387522} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1c7f4c-e3ef-5bde-9962-d7cea4dc6a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/01/01a8419fe9a435734f69c1913764423739b881fd6aa377a08f085e2ea7aab10b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387523} {:answer/id #uuid "4383caed-4158-5bd4-9a17-59fbd5c146d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9e/9ebbc28d7bf9b1ec3e5adb15a036570f1a7025d0dcedab7f61610c09b0c4c423.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387524} {:answer/id #uuid "5fce7b22-4627-502a-b774-fdb9a47268e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f17eeed2b5ab0b065ab0c4c7c7c669108ce4f0d8de8f69979316d12c2885d73e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387521} :answer-field/id #uuid "a762fefe-1fcc-5387-9367-a649dcf93ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e48d3f9e-79f6-5f88-9764-fe40dc9cde3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124569" :question/problem "![](images/de/deff964a1833339681fc38b30e32ebd3081e7ba1c4b5aabd0f4998f60a2aa78c.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,\\frac{v-1}{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{1-v}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/da/daefc9e14775a955e5210f965e474c06bc7cac1df9306941b73190cc8bf62219.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-3,-2),(-1,2),$ and $(0,2).$ \n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/94a2a90dae43d1edb1f0154a8c7ac60b8c35352fd72d5193014b9bba204d192f.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving."} :step/id #uuid "d663c1fb-e568-5737-ad46-ace94b52d55c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c730df9-6548-5576-ba64-558226099add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364915} {:answer/id #uuid "b366d454-2c12-5c37-a81b-f48f317a5394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364916} {:answer/id #uuid "86c803ea-012c-568d-90f8-bce9b0ec9560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364917} {:answer/id #uuid "cb699b26-8700-53e1-89a0-b258c6ab715c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364918} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a37d2a-7bd5-528f-9255-1cf91b830b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364917} :answer-field/id #uuid "3654e763-779e-5611-b4b5-b394a98f43a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91a59d9f-f2b5-5450-b850-f776f534f3f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124199" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $det(A)=-2$\n\nIII. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e086bf25-638a-5567-9fa8-97f5dc9d1a52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc4fab2-f286-5943-a45f-a20492b01ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417824} {:answer/id #uuid "76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417827} {:answer/id #uuid "ca24dbee-3bf2-5067-b768-9753b1ef80ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417831} {:answer/id #uuid "d2847d2c-57b6-59e8-b9ce-cd6f7d5c8fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417832} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b0cc75-4660-5f89-b375-205d3cc92d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417827} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6bf158-bc5a-5b83-aecb-e172b7a9b66e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "914af94d-7042-54e6-acb1-fed694c9a4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-162181" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe3a87c9-9231-55eb-8c5d-d5ab24f9c1a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435514}}] :activity/title "The Image of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435512}]}
